{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>The fear of HaShem adds days, but the years of the wicked are cut short (Proverbs 10:27).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, teaches us in this verse (Proverbs 10) that a person's years are lengthened or shortened according to merit and punishment, and this is by way of a hidden miracle. Now it is known that all miracles are divided into two categories: there are open miracles and there are hidden miracles. The open ones are the signs and great wonders performed before the eyes of the nations, such as the signs of Egypt in the ten plagues and in the splitting of the Sea of Reeds and the descent of the manna and the quails and the bringing forth of water from the rock — things that change the nature and custom of the world. The hidden ones are the deeds and events that befell the fathers Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, such as Abraham's war with those kings and his victory and prevailing over them — for even though it appears according to the custom of the world, it was all a hidden miracle. And likewise the addition of days to the righteous and the shortening of the days of the wicked — all is a hidden miracle. And of this Solomon said: יראת ה' תוסיף ימים — since worry and fear weaken a person's strength by way of nature and bring near the day of his death, Solomon said that whoever has fear of HaShem and worries about his sins — his days will be lengthened; and behold, this is a hidden miracle. ושנות רשעים תקצרנה — since pleasures broaden a person's heart by way of nature and extend and lengthen his days, therefore he says: the wicked man who indulges and is drawn after bodily pleasures — his pleasures will not avail him, for his years will be cut short, and this too is a hidden miracle. And this verse teaches you that length of days and shortness of days depend only on merit and punishment, in the manner that our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: in the First Temple only eighteen High Priests served, while in the Second Temple three hundred High Priests served — and this was because the priests in the First Temple were completely righteous and lived long, whereas the priests in the Second Temple corrupted their deeds and did not live long, and therefore there were so many of them." "<b>And behold,</b> this verse is not a categorical ruling that the righteous man's days are always lengthened and the wicked man's days always shortened, for we see wicked people living long and righteous people dying with short days. Rather, this verse explains the workings and the will of the Blessed Name at times — that He lengthens the days of the righteous, as He lengthened them for Hezekiah, and shortens the days of the wicked, as He did to Ben-Hadad king of Aram. And if He does not lengthen days for every righteous person and does not shorten the days of every wicked person, we may say that he is not completely righteous or he is not completely wicked; and if perhaps he is, then behold, this is to give them recompense according to the deeds of their hands in the World to Come, in order to magnify the reward for the righteous and to increase the punishment for the wicked. But either way, the addition and the shortening are nothing but a complete miracle, for the shortening of days before what was decreed and the addition of days beyond what was decreed — this is nothing but a complete miracle, and it is hidden from the eyes of creatures who see in it only the custom and nature of the world. And this matter is the foundation of the entire Torah, for a person has no portion in the Torah of Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, until he believes that all our deeds and all our events are hidden miracles, having no nature or custom of the world, neither for individuals nor for the multitudes; but when one performs a commandment its reward will prosper him, and when one commits a transgression its punishment will cut him down. And this is the substance of the Torah's promise in the blessings and the curses — for there is no single individual in Israel to whom hidden miracles do not occur every single day, and he does not know it; and so our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: the one who experiences the miracle does not recognize his miracle. And in order to demonstrate that hidden miracles are with a person every day, our Rabbis of blessed memory explained one teaching for us, namely what they said: one who enters to measure his threshing floor says, 'May it be Your will before You, HaShem my God and God of my fathers, that You send blessing in this heap'; and if he measured and afterward blessed, this is a vain prayer, for blessing is found neither in something measured nor in something counted nor in something weighed, but only in something hidden from the eye, as it is said (Deuteronomy 28:8): יצו ה' אתך את הברכה באסמיך — in something hidden from the eye. The Sages intended to say by this that hidden miracles occur every single day, and that they are in something not counted and not measured — for if the blessing took effect in what is counted, it would not be a hidden miracle but an open one, and not every person merits an open miracle. And to this intent they said: if he measured and afterward blessed, this is a vain prayer — since once it has been measured, a hidden miracle is impossible in it, and the evil eye holds sway over the very thing itself once measured, and likewise once weighed and counted. And for this reason the Torah commanded that Israel not be counted by their heads but by shekels, so that the blessing might rest upon them in the multiplication of their seed by way of a hidden miracle, and so that the evil eye — which holds sway over the very thing itself once counted — not hold sway over them. And since each and every one of Israel gives the ransom of his soul, a half-shekel, their number is known through the calculation of the half-shekels. And this is what is written:" "<b>When you lift up the head of the children of Israel for their census, they shall give each man the ransom of his soul to HaShem when you count them, and there shall be no plague among them when you count them.</b>" "<b>And by way of the plain sense (peshat):</b> he said כי תשא and did not express it in the manner of a commandment, in order to include in it both for the occasion and for the generations. For the fact that Scripture used the expression כי תשא teaches you that he shall count them now for this occasion and also includes further: that when he counts them for the generations he shall count them thus and not count them by their heads. And because the expression here was ambiguous and it was not specified whether the count was for the occasion or a commandment for the generations, therefore also King David erred and counted them without shekels — he it was who commanded Joab and said to him (2 Samuel 24:2): שוט נא בכל שבטי ישראל מדן ועד באר שבע ופקדו את העם וידעתי את מספר העם, and it is written: ואמר יואב אל המלך ויוסף ה' אלהיך אל העם כהם וכהם מאה פעמים ועיני אדוני המלך רואות ואדוני המלך למה חפץ בדבר הזה, and it is written: ויחזק דבר המלך אל יואב, and it is written: ויך לב דוד אותו אחרי כן ספר את העם, and David said to HaShem: חטאתי מאד אשר עשיתי." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'When you take' — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: By what shall you exalt the horn of Israel? By 'when you take,' as it is said (Proverbs 14:34): 'Righteousness exalts a nation, but the kindness of peoples is sin.' Rabbi Yehoshua says: 'Righteousness exalts a nation' — these are Israel, as it is said (II Samuel 7:23): 'And who is like Your people Israel, one nation in the land'; 'but the kindness of peoples is sin' — all the righteousness and kindness that the idolaters do, they do only to amplify their own greatness, as it is said (Daniel 4:24): 'Therefore, O king, let my counsel be acceptable to you, and atone for your sin by righteousness, and your iniquity by mercy to the poor — if there shall be length to your prosperity.'" "<b>And they shall give every man a ransom for his soul to HaShem, when you number them.</b> In the wilderness. <b>And there shall be no plague among them, when you number them.</b> For all generations; therefore He mentioned 'when you number them' a second time. Or the first instance is in the sense of counting and the second in the sense of providential scrutiny, in the manner of (Jeremiah 10:15) 'at the time of their visitation they shall perish'; and the measure of the verse is thus: 'And they shall give every man a ransom for his soul to HaShem, when counting them, so that there shall be no plague among them, when scrutinizing them.'" "<b>And it was revealed</b> to you by Scripture that the people, when particularized in their counting, each one to his own skull, is thereby subjected to the providence of the Master of providence, may He be exalted, and all his deeds are particularized before Him, and then the plague may come into effect — unlike the prior state, when his affair was subsumed within the generality of the many; but now, with each individual singled out and all his deeds particularized, punishment is unavoidable. And like the matter that the Shunammite woman answered Elisha (II Kings 4:13): 'I dwell among my own people,' and so forth — meaning: I do not want you to pray in my name before the king or before the commander of the army; it is better for me that my affair be included within the generality of the many and not be particularized, lest I be punished. And it is known that that day of which it is written (there): 'And the day came' — that was Rosh HaShanah. And from here it is fitting that on that day, when all the inhabitants of the world pass before Him like the children of Maron, it should be a day of judgment and visitation of punishment upon the world, and to be seized with fear and anxiety on it — since every individual is particularized and all his deeds are particularized for each man according to his ways and according to the fruit of his doings. And so you find in the book of Ruth (Ruth 1:1): 'And a man went from Bethlehem of Judah' — Scripture mentioned him anonymously, and when he was particularized afterward and named, 'and the name of the man was Elimelech,' what is written there? 'And Elimelech, husband of Naomi, died.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :register {:flags {2 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:06.454836393Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}